{"title":"Adding fractions with different denominators","key_idea":"To add fractions, the bottom numbers must be the same, so you find a common denominator first.","method":["Look at the denominators (bottom numbers). Here they are 4 and 8.","Find the smallest number that both 4 and 8 divide into evenly. That is 8.","Change 3/4 to have 8 as the denominator: multiply top and bottom by 2, giving 6/8.","Now add: 6/8 + 1/8 = 7/8."],"worked_example":{"problem":"Add 3/4 and 1/8","steps":["Denominators are 4 and 8. Common denominator is 8.","Convert 3/4: multiply by 2/2 to get 6/8.","Add the numerators: 6 + 1 = 7.","Keep the denominator: 7/8."],"answer":"7/8"},"key_terms":[{"term":"denominator","meaning":"the bottom number of a fraction"},{"term":"common denominator","meaning":"a number that both denominators divide into evenly"},{"term":"numerator","meaning":"the top number of a fraction"}],"common_mistakes":["Adding the denominators too (4 + 8) instead of keeping them the same.","Forgetting to multiply the top number when you change the denominator."],"remember":"Match the bottoms first, then add the tops.","quick_check":[{"q":"What is the common denominator for 3/4 and 1/8?","a":"8"},{"q":"What is 3/4 equal to when written with denominator 8?","a":"6/8"}],"tell_your_child":"We need to make the fractions speak the same language before we add them. Once the bottoms match, we just add the tops and keep the bottom the same."}
